0. Research Mission

This dissertation advances a formal theoretical framework for the design of a Civilization Code: a physically embodied, geographically distributed, and self-explaining archive system capable of preserving and transmitting essential human knowledge across a civilizational discontinuity spanning $10^4$ to $10^6$ years. The motivating scenario assumes a planetary-scale catastrophe eliminates modern technological civilization, leaving only small, isolated human populations whose descendants, generations later, live at pre-industrial technological levels and possess no continuity of modern language, mathematics, computing, or institutional memory.

The archive must therefore solve a problem categorically more difficult than ordinary data preservation: How can one civilization transmit knowledge to another civilization that lacks every conceptual, linguistic, technological, and institutional prerequisite required to read that knowledge? This reframes the challenge from an information storage problem to a civilizational engineering problem. The goal is not merely to preserve data but to preserve the possibility of civilizational reconstitution.

Draft v0.4 incorporates three fundamental theoretical advances arising from adversarial review of Draft v0.3. First, we establish a category-theoretic bridge—formalized as a full and faithful monoidal functor—connecting tactile mechanical primitives to abstract formal syntax, thereby eliminating the cognitive gap between perceptual grounding and symbolic representation. Second, we reformulate the physical preservation layer as a spatially correlated loss channel, introducing multi-scale interleaved coding schemes that guarantee decodability under non-uniform geological erosion. Third, we embed game-theoretic mechanism design into the archive architecture, ensuring that cooperative decoding constitutes the unique strong Nash equilibrium among surviving populations.

