10.1 The Asynchronous Physical Degradation Channel

A central vulnerability of physical information preservation over multi-millennial horizons ($10^4$ to $10^6$ years) is the breakdown of classical memoryless noise assumptions. Standard telecommunication channel models rely on additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) or memoryless binary symmetric channel (BSC) formulations. However, in deep-time geological preservation, physical substrates undergo non-stationary, spatially correlated degradation driven by micro-fracture propagation, radiation-induced defect cascade, chemical devitrification, and localized mechanical shear.

Rather than experiencing uniform bit-flip noise, the storage medium acts as an Asynchronous Physical Loss Channel with Spatial Memory. Physical damage manifests as concentrated, high-density burst erasures surrounded by regions of relative structural integrity. If an information encoding scheme assumes independent error distribution, a single localized fracture line across a physical disk will destroy contiguous codeword segments, causing complete decoding failure regardless of global signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).

To guarantee that the Universal Information Representation (C4 UIR) remains reconstructible under severe physical erosion, the channel coding framework must decouple local spatial damage from code-space distance.

10.2 Non-Uniform Spatial Erosion Modeling

We model the spatial degradation of a 3D physical storage substrate $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ over a time horizon $T \in [10^4, 10^6]\text{ years}$ as a spatial Markov-Gaussian random field.

Definition 10.1 (Spatial Defect Density Field).
Let $\eta(\mathbf{x}, t) \in [0, 1]$ denote the physical defect density at coordinate $\mathbf{x} = (x, y, z) \in \Omega$ at time $t$. The spatial evolution of $\eta(\mathbf{x}, t)$ is governed by a stochastic partial differential equation combining uniform thermal/diffusive degradation with localized catastrophic burst events:

\eta(\mathbf{x}, t) = \eta_0 \cdot e^{\lambda_{\text{bg}} t} + \sum_{k=1}^{N_{\text{defects}}(t)} A_k \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{\Vert{}\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_k\Vert{}^2}{2\sigma_k^2} \right)

where:

· $\eta_0$ is the initial manufacturing defect density.
· $\lambda_{\text{bg}}$ is the isotropic background material decay rate (e.g., thermal diffusion in glass).
· $N_{\text{defects}}(t) \sim \text{Poisson}(\Lambda t)$ models the cumulative arrival of physical disruption events (cosmic ray tracks, seismic shock micro-cracks, spallation).
· $\mathbf{x}_k \sim \text{Uniform}(\Omega)$ is the spatial centroid of damage event $k$.
· $A_k \in (0, 1]$ and $\sigma_k > 0$ represent the peak damage intensity and spatial correlation length of event $k$, respectively.

Definition 10.2 (Physical Voxel Bit Error Probability).
Let $Y(\mathbf{x}) \in \{0, 1\}$ be the retrieved bit state from a physical voxel located at $\mathbf{x}$. The conditional bit error probability $P_e(\mathbf{x}, t) = P(Y(\mathbf{x}) \neq X(\mathbf{x}))$ is defined via a non-linear threshold mapping:

