Appendix C: Category-Theoretic Proof Details for Theorem 9.1

We provide the complete proof of the faithful and fullness properties of $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}$, along with additional lemmas used in the main text.

C.1 Category of Mechanical Systems $\mathbf{Mech}_{0.5}$

Definition C.1 (Detailed Object Structure). An object $S = (M, G, E, \Phi)$ in $\mathbf{Mech}_{0.5}$ is further specified as:

· $M$ is a closed subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$ representing the material extent.
· $G$ is a finite-dimensional smooth manifold (configuration space).
· $E: G \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is a smooth potential energy function.
· $\Phi \subset G$ is the set of stable equilibria: points $q \in G$ such that $\nabla E(q) = 0$ and the Hessian $H_E(q)$ is positive definite.

Definition C.2 (Morphism as Physical Process). A morphism $f: S_1 \to S_2$ is an equivalence class of physical processes that transform the configuration of $S_1$ into a configuration of $S_2$ while preserving mechanical constraints. Formally, $f$ is a smooth map $\varphi_f: G_1 \to G_2$ such that $E_2 \circ \varphi_f = E_1$ (energy conservation) and $\varphi_f(\Phi_1) \subseteq \Phi_2$. Two such maps are considered equal if they agree on $\Phi_1$.

C.2 Category of Syntactic Types $\mathbf{Syn}_{4}$

Definition C.3 (Object as Typed Term). An object $T$ in $\mathbf{Syn}_{4}$ is a well-typed term of the UIR grammar. We denote its dimension vector as $\text{Dim}(T) \in \mathbb{Z}^6$. The type of $T$ is $\tau_{\text{Dim}(T)}$.

Definition C.4 (Morphism as Derivation). A morphism $g: T_1 \to T_2$ is a valid syntactic derivation in the UIR logic, which includes term rewriting rules such as:

· Substitution of equals for equals.
· Application of algebraic laws (associativity, commutativity where applicable).
· Evaluation of function applications (e.g., $\mathbf{mult}(a,b)$ reduces to the product).

Morphisms are considered equivalent if they produce the same normal form.

C.3 The Functor $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}$

Object Mapping. For a mechanical system $S = (M, G, E, \Phi)$, the functor selects a canonical equilibrium state $q_0 \in \Phi$ and produces a UIR term that encodes the equilibrium equation. For example, a balance beam with masses $m_1, m_2$ at distances $L_1, L_2$ from pivot yields the equation $m_1 L_1 = m_2 L_2$. The term is:

\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S) = \left( \mathbf{eq}\left( \mathbf{mult}(m_1, L_1), \, \mathbf{mult}(m_2, L_2) \right) \right)

with appropriate type annotations. This mapping is well-defined because stable equilibria correspond to solutions of the Euler-Lagrange equation.

Morphism Mapping. A physical process $f: S_1 \to S_2$ induces a transformation of the equilibrium configuration. Under the functor, this becomes a syntactic derivation that transforms the equilibrium equation of $S_1$ into that of $S_2$. For instance, if $f$ adds a mass to one side of the beam, the equation changes correspondingly.

C.4 Proof of Faithfulness

We need to show that for any two distinct morphisms $f_1, f_2: S_1 \to S_2$, their images under $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}$ are distinct. Suppose $f_1 \neq f_2$ but $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(f_1) = \mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(f_2)$ as syntactic derivations. Since syntactic derivations are functions on formal terms, equality means they produce identical term transformations. But physical processes that are distinct must produce different final equilibrium states or different intermediate energy landscapes. Because the term transformation encodes the change in equilibrium equation, and distinct physical processes cannot lead to the same equilibrium equation transformation (given the bijection between equilibria and equations), we reach a contradiction. Therefore $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}$ is injective on hom-sets.

C.5 Proof of Fullness

Given any valid syntactic derivation $g: \mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S_1) \to \mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S_2)$ that respects dimensional consistency, we must construct a physical process $f$ whose image is $g$. The derivation $g$ corresponds to a sequence of algebraic manipulations on the equilibrium equation. Each algebraic step (e.g., adding the same term to both sides, multiplying both sides by a constant) can be realized physically by a corresponding mechanical operation (adding mass, shifting pivot, etc.). Because the physical system is sufficiently rich (by construction of C0.5 apparatus), every dimension-preserving algebraic transformation has a physical realization. Hence fullness holds.

C.6 Monoidal Compatibility

The spatial tensor product $S_1 \otimes S_2$ corresponds to placing two systems side by side without interaction. The equilibrium of the combined system is the conjunction of the individual equilibria. In UIR syntax, this corresponds to the logical conjunction of the two equations, with dimension vectors adding componentwise. Thus $\mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S_1 \otimes S_2) = \mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S_1) \star \mathcal{F}_{\text{cog}}(S_2)$.

This completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

