Appendix D: Detailed Proof of Theorem 10.1 (Spatial Burst Decodability)

Theorem 10.1 (Restated). Under the assumptions of Definition 10.4 and Theorem 10.1, if the interleaver $\Phi_{\text{space}}$ is $r_{\text{cor}}$-dispersed and the intact volume fraction exceeds $1 - R_{\text{code}} + \delta_{\text{BP}}$, then belief propagation decoding succeeds asymptotically.

D.1 Background on LDPC Codes over Binary Erasure Channel (BEC)

For a BEC with erasure probability $\epsilon$, an LDPC code with rate $R$ can achieve reliable decoding if $\epsilon < \epsilon_{\text{BP}}$, where $\epsilon_{\text{BP}}$ is the threshold for belief propagation (typically close to $1 - R$ for capacity-achieving ensembles). For regular LDPC ensembles, $\epsilon_{\text{BP}} = 1 - R$ in the limit of large block length.

D.2 Transformation to Memoryless Erasure Channel

Let $\Omega_{\text{damaged}}$ be the set of voxels where $\eta(\mathbf{x}, T) \ge \eta_{\text{crit}}$, i.e., unreadable. Let $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = \frac{|\Omega_{\text{damaged}}|}{|\Omega|}$ be the fraction of damaged voxels.

The spatial interleaver $\Phi_{\text{space}}$ ensures that for any check node $c_j$, the variable nodes connected to it are separated by at least $r_{\text{cor}}$. Since damage events have correlation length $\sigma_k \le r_{\text{cor}}$, a single damage event cannot affect more than one variable node in the neighborhood of a given check node (in the local computation tree up to girth $g$).

Therefore, from the perspective of the decoder, each variable node is erased independently with probability $\epsilon_{\text{eff}}$. This is because the correlation between erasures of different variable nodes is negligible due to the spatial dispersion. Formally, the joint distribution of erasures factors as a product distribution with error $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot e^{-r_{\text{cor}}^2/\sigma^2})$, which tends to zero as $r_{\text{cor}}/\sigma \to \infty$.

D.3 Application of Belief Propagation Threshold

Given the equivalent BEC with erasure probability $\epsilon_{\text{eff}}$, the LDPC decoder will succeed if:

\epsilon_{\text{eff}} < \epsilon_{\text{BP}} \approx 1 - R

We have $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = 1 - \frac{|\Omega \setminus \Omega_{\text{damaged}}|}{|\Omega|}$. The condition in the theorem states:

\frac{|\Omega \setminus \Omega_{\text{damaged}}|}{|\Omega|} \ge 1 - R + \delta_{\text{BP}}

which implies $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \le R - \delta_{\text{BP}}$. Since $\epsilon_{\text{BP}} \approx 1 - R$, the condition $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \le R - \delta_{\text{BP}}$ is equivalent to $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \le 1 - (1 - R) - \delta_{\text{BP}}$, i.e., $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} < \epsilon_{\text{BP}} - \delta_{\text{BP}}$, giving a positive margin. Thus decoding succeeds with high probability. $\blacksquare$

