Appendix E: Proof of Theorem 10.2 (Capacity Limit)

Theorem 10.2 (Restated). The physical archive footprint $V_{\text{archive}}$ guarantees $P(U \mid D) \to 1.0$ if and only if:

V_{\text{archive}} \ge \frac{I_{\text{MVCD}}}{C_{\text{deep\_time}}(10^5\text{ years})} \cdot (1 + \gamma_{\text{margin}})

E.1 Capacity Derivation

From Definition 10.5, the deep-time capacity per unit volume is:

C_{\text{deep\_time}}(T) = D_{\text{vol}} \cdot \left( 1 - \iiint_{\Omega_0} H_2(P_e(\mathbf{x}, T)) \, d\mathbf{x} \right) \cdot \rho_{\text{interleave}}^{-1}

The factor $\left( 1 - \iiint H_2(P_e) \, d\mathbf{x} \right)$ represents the average fraction of voxels that remain reliable (information-theoretically). Under the assumption that $P_e(\mathbf{x}, T)$ is either near 0 (intact) or near 1 (erased), the integral reduces to the damaged fraction $\epsilon_{\text{eff}}$, and the capacity becomes:

C_{\text{deep\_time}} \approx D_{\text{vol}} \cdot (1 - \epsilon_{\text{eff}}) \cdot \rho_{\text{interleave}}^{-1}

But from Theorem 10.1, for successful decoding we require $\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \le R - \delta_{\text{BP}} \le R$, where $R$ is the code rate. The interleaver redundancy factor $\rho_{\text{interleave}}$ accounts for the fact that we must over-provision physical voxels to achieve the desired logical information density. The effective logical information density is:

D_{\text{logical}} = D_{\text{vol}} \cdot R \cdot \rho_{\text{interleave}}^{-1}

If we set $R = 1 - \epsilon_{\text{eff}} - \delta_{\text{BP}}$, then to store $I_{\text{MVCD}}$ bits, we need total volume:

V = \frac{I_{\text{MVCD}}}{D_{\text{logical}}} = \frac{I_{\text{MVCD}}}{D_{\text{vol}} \cdot (1 - \epsilon_{\text{eff}}) \cdot \rho_{\text{interleave}}^{-1}}

which matches the definition of $C_{\text{deep\_time}}$ when $\epsilon_{\text{eff}}$ is the damaged fraction. The margin factor $\gamma_{\text{margin}}$ accounts for boundary effects, macro-fractures that are larger than correlation length, and other non-ideal losses. $\blacksquare$

