Appendix F: Game-Theoretic Proof of Theorem 16.1

Theorem 16.1 (Restated). Cooperative Reconstitution is the unique Strong Nash Equilibrium in the Multi-Key Vault game.

F.1 Formal Game Model

Let $N = \{1, 2, \ldots, n\}$ be the set of tribes, each controlling a site containing one key fragment. The vault requires $m \le n$ distinct keys to open. Payoffs:

· If tribes cooperate and open the vault, each receives utility $U_i(\text{Cooperate}) = V_{\text{Civilization\_Stack}} / n$.
· If a tribe chooses to monopolize (refuse to share its key or attempt to seize others' keys), the vault cannot be opened by that tribe alone. Even if a tribe acquires $k < m$ keys, the knowledge remains inaccessible. The utility from monopolization is limited to the primitive material value of the site itself, $V_{\text{primitive}}$, minus conflict costs $C_{\text{conflict}}$.

We assume $V_{\text{Civilization\_Stack}} \gg n \cdot (V_{\text{primitive}} - C_{\text{conflict}})$.

F.2 Definition of Strong Nash Equilibrium

A strategy profile $s^* = (s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)$ is a Strong Nash Equilibrium if no coalition $S \subseteq N$ can deviate to a new strategy profile that strictly improves the payoff of every member of $S$.

F.3 Proof

Consider any strategy profile that does not result in full cooperation (i.e., vault not opened). In such a profile, each tribe's payoff is at most $V_{\text{primitive}} - C_{\text{conflict}}$ (if they fight) or $0$ (if they refrain). Since $V_{\text{Civilization\_Stack}} / n \gg V_{\text{primitive}} - C_{\text{conflict}}$, any coalition that can enforce cooperation would improve all members' payoffs. However, cooperation requires all $m$ key holders to agree. If any subset holds out, the vault remains closed. The only way a coalition can guarantee improvement is if it consists of all players, in which case the grand coalition can open the vault and distribute benefits, strictly improving everyone. Therefore, the only Strong Nash Equilibrium is the cooperative profile where all tribes share keys.

Moreover, due to the threshold nature of the gate, no strict subset of players can unilaterally open the vault; thus no smaller coalition can improve by deviating from cooperation. This establishes uniqueness. $\blacksquare$

F.4 Robustness to Coalition Formation

Even if some tribes form a coalition to exclude others, they still need $m$ keys. If they exclude others but have $m$ among themselves, they can open the vault and potentially monopolize the knowledge. However, the physical design can require that the keys must be activated simultaneously from geographically separated sites, making it impossible for a subset to physically gather all keys without cooperation of the other sites (e.g., because each key must be inserted at its own site, and the vault is located elsewhere, requiring simultaneous transmission). This ensures that only a coalition that includes all necessary site controllers can succeed, effectively forcing grand cooperation.

